name: reachability
boss: Portbreaker
flavor: I count the doors before you knock.
brief: Four questions, in order, when something will not connect: is the link
brief: up, does it have an address, is there a route, does dns work. Answer
brief: them in that order and you will almost never guess.
brief:
brief: ping proves a host answers. traceroute shows where the packets stop.
brief: netstat shows what your own machine is doing about it.
---
q: send three pings to @ip@ and stop
a: ping -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -c
---
q: ping @ip@ quietly, printing only the summary
a: ping -c 3 -q @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -q
---
q: ping with one second between packets
a: ping -i 1 -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -i
---
q: ping out of a specific interface
a: ping -I @iface@ -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -I
---
q: ping with a payload of 1000 bytes to test for MTU trouble
a: ping -s 1000 -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -s
---
q: give up on the whole ping run after 5 seconds
a: ping -w 5 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -w
---
q: wait at most 2 seconds for each individual reply
a: ping -W 2 -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -W
---
q: ping with a fixed time to live of 5 hops
a: ping -t 5 -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -t
---
q: ping using a chosen pattern in the payload bytes
a: ping -p ff -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -p
---
q: ping in adaptive mode, sending as fast as replies come back
a: ping -A -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -A
---
q: %show% the path packets take to @ip@
a: traceroute @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute
---
q: trace the path without resolving names, so it runs fast
a: traceroute -n @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -n
---
q: trace no further than 10 hops
a: traceroute -m 10 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -m
---
q: send one probe per hop instead of three
a: traceroute -q 1 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -q
---
q: start the trace at hop 5, skipping the first four
a: traceroute -f 5 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -f
---
q: trace using icmp echo rather than udp
a: traceroute -I @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -I
---
q: trace out of a specific interface
a: traceroute -i @iface@ @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -i
---
q: trace using a chosen source address
a: traceroute -s @ip2@ @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -s
---
q: trace to a specific destination port
a: traceroute -p @port@ @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -p
---
q: wait at most 2 seconds for each probe
a: traceroute -w 2 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -w
---
q: trace verbosely, showing every packet received
a: traceroute -v @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -v
---
q: trace bypassing the normal routing tables
a: traceroute -r @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -r
---
q: pause half a second between probes
a: traceroute -z 500 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -z
---
q: set the time to live of the first probe
a: traceroute -t 1 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -t
---
q: set the probe packet length
a: traceroute -l 60 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -l
---
q: do not fragment the trace packets
a: traceroute -F @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -F
---
q: look up the address of a hostname
a: nslookup @host@
ref: nslookup
teach: nslookup
---
q: look up a name using a specific dns server
a: nslookup @host@ @ip@
ref: nslookup
teach: nslookup
---
q: what sockets is this machine using
a: netstat
ref: netstat
teach: netstat
---
q: %show% only listening sockets
a: netstat -l
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -l
---
q: %show% every socket, listening or not
a: netstat -a
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -a
---
q: %show% sockets numerically, without resolving names or ports
a: netstat -n
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -n
---
q: %show% only tcp sockets
a: netstat -t
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
---
q: %show% only udp sockets
a: netstat -u
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -u
---
q: %show% which process owns each socket
a: netstat -p
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -p
---
q: %show% every listening tcp socket numerically, with its process
a: netstat -tlnp
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
---
q: %show% the routing table using netstat
a: netstat -r
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -r
---
q: %show% unix domain sockets
a: netstat -x
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -x
---
q: %show% raw sockets
a: netstat -w
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -w
---
q: %show% netstat output with extended information
a: netstat -e
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -e
---
q: %show% netstat with wide columns so long addresses are not truncated
a: netstat -W
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -W
---
q: ping an ipv6 host three times
a: ping6 -c 3 ::1
ref: ping6
teach: ping6
hint: the whole world is moving to ipv6; the tools have a 6 on the end
---
q: trace the ipv6 path to a host
a: traceroute6 ::1
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6
---
q: trace the ipv6 path without resolving names
a: traceroute6 -n ::1
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6 -n
---
q: trace an ipv6 path no further than 10 hops
a: traceroute6 -m 10 ::1
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6 -m
